<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Gisela of Bavaria</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 985–<a href="/years/1065">1065</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a> · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottonian-dynasty">Ottonian dynasty</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Gisela of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria">Bavaria</a> was the first queen of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a>, wife of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stephen-king-of-england">King Stephen</a> I and sister of Emperor Henry II. Her marriage around 996 tied the new Christian Hungarian monarchy to the Ottonian Empire and brought Bavarian clergy and knights to Hungary. After Stephen's death in 1038 she was mistreated under King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peter-orseolo">Peter Orseolo</a> and returned to Bavaria, dying as abbess of Niedernburg in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/passau">Passau</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/gisela-of-bavaria.jpg" alt="Gisela of Bavaria" width="320" height="411" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Portrayal_of_Gisela_of_Hungary_on_the_coronation_pall-2.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Gisela was a daughter of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-ii-duke-of-bavaria">Henry II, Duke of Bavaria</a>, and Gisela of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burgundy">Burgundy</a>, and is also known as Gisela of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a>. She received a solid Catholic education, most likely with Bishop <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wolfgang-of-regensburg">Wolfgang of Regensburg</a> as her teacher. In 996 she married King Stephen I of Hungary as part of Hungary's policy of opening up to the West. The wedding marked a turning point in Hungarian history. The couple had a son, Saint Emeric, who died on 2 September 1031 while hunting boar.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>As queen, Gisela did much to spread the Christian faith and Western culture in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a>. She also took part in the dynastic affairs of the wider region. When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edmund-ironside">Edmund Ironside</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> died and was succeeded by Cnut, Edmund's infant sons were sent abroad and ended up under the protection of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stephen-king-of-england">King Stephen</a>. One of the twins died young, but the other, Edward Atheling, was brought up as a protégé of Queen Gisela, and was regarded at that foreign court as heir to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anglo-saxons">Anglo-Saxon</a> throne. Gisela also commissioned a jewelled cross for the tomb of her mother, who died in 1006 and was buried in the Niedermünster in Regensburg.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Stephen died in 1038. In 1046 Gisela, her attendants and a number of Bavarian settlers left <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a> to return to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria">Bavaria</a>, where she joined the Niedernburg convent in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/passau">Passau</a> and became its abbess. She died there. Her grave is well known and regarded as a holy place.</p><p>An attempt to canonise her in the 18th century failed, and she was beatified in 1975. Her memorial days are 7 May and 1 February. On 4 May 1996 a bone from her arm was brought to Veszprém, where she once lived, together with the preserved right hand of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stephen-king-of-england">King Stephen</a> for a mass marking the thousandth anniversary of their wedding.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Gisela of Hungary” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Stephen married <b>Gisela of Bavaria</b>, sister of the future Emperor Henry II, and on his father's death in 997 defeated his kinsman Koppány, who claimed the succession by seniority.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-hungary-1000-1301">Kingdom of Hungary (1000-1301) · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Géza was baptised and married his son Vajk to <b>Gisela of Bavaria</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/principality-of-hungary">Principality of Hungary</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Lengfeld, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. Died in Kloster Niedernburg, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=48.92,12.01&amp;year=1037" aria-label="Lengfeld on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4827 456 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Gisela of Bavaria"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5277 726h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5277 726h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=48.92,12.01&amp;year=1037">What was here in 1037? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
